WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.209
<v Jamie>Meta's burning a billion dollars building its own AI chips while the stock sinks—apparently the way to prove you're serious about the future is to spend like you're desperate about the present.

00:00:10.209 --> 00:00:33.104
<v Alex>Small caps are having a moment—the Russell up one point two percent while the S&P crawls forward at zero point four eight. A M D jumped seven point two five percent, Oracle up three point nine five, Cisco three point nine three. Meanwhile Costco dropped four point six five percent and Pepsi is down four, so the market's basically saying: sure, buy semiconductors, but if you need toilet paper or soda you're overpaying.

00:00:33.104 --> 00:00:43.623
<v Jamie>Wait, so the consumer names are getting hammered while the chip stocks moon. Is this the AI capex crowd finally rotating out of the mega-cap names, or is this just Costco being expensive?

00:00:43.623 --> 00:01:07.755
<v Alex>Costco's been expensive since the Eisenhower administration, but yeah—the Nasdaq is up zero point six four percent, which is fine, except all of that gain is being hoarded by the infrastructure play. Google down two point three four, Salesforce down two point zero two. The money that was chasing AI chips last month is now chasing the companies that *make* the chips, and the companies selling you stuff at retail are getting left in the parking lot.

00:01:07.755 --> 00:01:25.699
<v Maya>So we've got this weird split where the entire economy is supposedly booming but people are apparently not buying bulk paper towels anymore, which feels like the canary that actually matters. The V I X is down two point four nine, everyone's calm, but Pepsi losing four percent in a day—that's not a shrug, that's a message.

00:01:25.699 --> 00:01:42.406
<v Maya>So the thing nobody's talking about is the timeline. Meta's not just sketching this on a napkin—they've already taped out their first in-house chip, and it's supposed to ship later this year. That's not a press release, that's a manufacturing commitment. They're betting the entire AI infrastructure play on silicon they designed themselves.

00:01:42.406 --> 00:01:53.853
<v Alex>Which is smart from a cost and latency standpoint, except investors are reading it as a confession. If you need to build your own chips to stay competitive, you're admitting Nvidia's got you by the throat.

00:01:53.853 --> 00:02:03.444
<v Jamie>But Meta's already spending what, eighteen billion a year on capex? Adding a whole semiconductor division on top of that—how much more runway does this burn before it pays off?

00:02:03.444 --> 00:02:22.007
<v Maya>That's the trap they're in. Vertical integration takes five, six years to show real margin improvement. Wall Street wants proof of life now. So Meta's stuck in this weird zone where the smart long-term move looks like panic spending to the quarterly-earnings crowd. It's like they're building a factory while everyone's watching the construction costs and ignoring the product.

00:02:22.007 --> 00:02:43.973
<v Alex>And the second-order problem is that Nvidia's not sitting still. Every dollar Meta spends on in-house silicon is a dollar that's not buying Nvidia chips, which means Nvidia has to keep innovating faster just to justify staying expensive. So Meta's vertical move actually accelerates the entire chip arms race—everyone else gets spooked and starts thinking about their own designs or hedging with A M D. The market hates that uncertainty.

00:02:43.973 --> 00:02:57.586
<v Alex>PepsiCo just reported earnings and they're in a weird spot—they cut prices to compete, volume still tanked, so now they're making less money per bottle and selling fewer bottles. The consumer is not coming back for cheaper Doritos. They're just buying less Doritos.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/09/pepsico-pep-q2-2026-earnings.html

00:02:57.586 --> 00:03:03.155
<v Jamie>So they're trapped. Lower prices don't unlock demand, they just erode margin. What does international look like?

00:03:03.155 --> 00:03:11.508
<v Alex>International's fine, which is the problem—it makes the domestic story look like a choice. U.S. consumers are actually pulling back, not just waiting for a sale.

00:03:11.508 --> 00:03:25.740
<v Maya>Meta's also jumping into the AI coding market now. So they're building their own chips, chasing the infrastructure play, AND now they're trying to compete with Anthropic and OpenAI on the software side. They're basically trying to own every layer of the stack at once.
  ↳ source: https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/09/anthropic-openai-and-spacex-are-bigger-than-the-last-25-years-of-tech-exits/

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:34.403
<v Jamie>That's the move though, right? If you're Meta, you can't just be a chip company or just a model company. You have to be both or you lose.

00:03:34.403 --> 00:03:46.159
<v Maya>Yeah, but now they're competing with three different sets of enemies—Nvidia on chips, OpenAI on models, and Amazon on cloud infrastructure. That's not diversification, that's just picking three separate fights and hoping one of them goes viral.

00:03:46.159 --> 00:03:59.463
<v Alex>Tanker traffic through the Strait of Hormuz is slowing after the Iranian attacks. Oil rallied on supply concerns, but Fed Vice Chair Williams said don't expect a sustained price surge. He's basically saying the market's panicking over something that won't stick around.
  ↳ source: https://seekingalpha.com/news/4612686-when-will-traffic-at-the-strait-of-hormuz-return-to-normal?utm_source=feed_news_all&utm_medium=referral&feed_item_type=news

00:03:59.463 --> 00:04:07.507
<v Jamie>So the Fed's already pre-emptively managing the narrative on energy prices. They're worried the market will overshoot and then they'll have to mop it up.

00:04:07.507 --> 00:04:19.573
<v Jamie>Trump says Iran called to make a deal after the U.S. strikes. Also says it's unclear if war is back on. So we've got a potential ceasefire and also maybe a full-scale conflict, and nobody knows which one.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/09/trump-iran-war-strikes-hormuz.html

00:04:19.573 --> 00:04:27.926
<v Maya>Farage's by-election in Clacton is officially confirmed. The man already won the seat, so now he's running again because apparently one Parliament wasn't enough real estate.
  ↳ source: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwylzxw7p52o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss

00:04:27.926 --> 00:04:32.876
<v Jamie>Wait, he already won Clacton. Why is there a by-election for a seat he's holding?

00:04:32.876 --> 00:04:45.561
<v Alex>Because the writ was moved in the Commons—Reform's way of saying Farage didn't actually want to show up to Parliament, so they're just running the same election again in case he changes his mind about the whole legislative branch thing.
  ↳ source: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwylzxw7p52o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss

00:04:45.561 --> 00:04:48.036
<v Jamie>On the tape tomorrow—what are we watching?

00:04:48.036 --> 00:05:01.340
<v Alex>The big one's still the inflation picture. C P I's due Friday, and after the Fed's already signaling they're not sweating the oil spike, this number either confirms they're right to relax or it blows up their whole 'transitory energy shock' thesis.

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:21.140
<v Maya>Meanwhile Cable One reports earnings tomorrow—they're a regional broadband and video provider, so basically the canary in the coal mine for whether people are still paying for internet and cable bundles or if cord-cutting finally hit the point where it breaks the economics. Given what we just saw with Pepsi, I'm genuinely curious if the consumer pullback shows up in their subscriber numbers.

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:28.256
<v Maya>Meta's spending eighteen billion a year to avoid needing Nvidia—which is like paying for a house to prove you don't need landlords.

00:05:28.256 --> 00:05:31.969
<v Alex>And the landlord's still raising rent while they're swinging a hammer.

00:05:31.969 --> 00:05:36.300
<v Jamie>This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
